From 54652608f6cd041b7d9932ee6e4da47f37fbff87 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 21:54:01 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08076/11362] [outer-loop] mark which GameAdapters replay a
 stored plan vs search an action space

18 of 25 replay. Their action_labels hand back the next label of a banked plan
(s5i5: one fixed click), so solve_adaptered's verifier-routed search, hazard
pruning, learned-verifier warm start and state dedup never engage -- the search
is a straight line through a solution that was already known.

The distinction was invisible without reading each adapter's source, which is why
it took three askings to surface. It also makes downstream numbers wrong: a
replay adapter "reaching L1" in ops/arc_adapter_depth_baseline.json measures the
STORED PLAN, not adapter capability, and nothing said so.

Adds GameAdapter.replay: bool and replay_source: str. The source is required when
replay=True so the claim is traceable to an artifact or constant rather than
asserted -- wa30 names the gate-verified probe route, sc25 names
exp4468.SC25_PLANS_BY_LEVEL[1], g50t records that it was registered by Exp4884
only after adapter-free L2 search dead-ended.

Classification is PINNED, not derived, and the docstring says why: branching
factor cannot separate the classes. tn36 also returns a single label at L0 but
COMPUTES it from live state, so it survives a layout change where a stored plan
would not. There is no cheap runtime predicate for "derived vs read from a list".

The one empirical check that IS available is included:
test_replay_adapters_run_out_at_l0 exhausts each plan and asserts nothing remains
at L0. 17 of 18 return ZERO labels. That is the finding behind the whole
question -- removing the plan does not reveal a search space underneath, because
the plan IS the L0 behaviour, which is why "upgrade them to genuine adapters" is
per-game RE rather than un-bypassing something already written. s5i5 is exempt
and the exemption is recorded rather than the case being forced into the pattern.

6 tests. No behaviour change: both fields default to the search classification,
so nothing reads differently unless it asks.
